<TopicPage slug="subjects/criminology/foundational/changing-awareness-of-crime">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Unit 1 of the WJEC Level 3 Applied Diploma in Criminology starts from an uncomfortable
    observation: <strong>what the public "knows" about crime is mostly manufactured</strong> —
    by what gets reported, what gets recorded, and what the media chooses to dramatise. Some
    crimes dominate the headlines; others are so hidden, so normalised, or so "complex" that
    nobody reports them at all. This lesson walks the unit's first four assessment criteria:
    the types of crime (AC1.1), why some go unreported and what that costs (AC1.2–1.3), how
    media represents crime (AC1.4), and how we even measure the thing (AC1.6).
  </p>

  <h2>Types of crime you must analyse</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>White-collar crime</strong> — committed by the respectable in the course of work: organised, corporate and professional variants. Fraud, tax evasion, price-fixing. Often deviant and criminal at once, and routinely underestimated because its victims are diffuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Moral crimes</strong> — acts against perceived standards of decency: prostitution, vagrancy, illegal gambling, assisted suicide. "Victimless" is contested, and that contest drives whether anyone calls the police.</li>
    <li><strong>State crimes</strong> — offences by or with the acquiescence of governments: genocide, torture, imprisonment without trial, human-rights violations. The offender writes the rules, so awareness depends on outsiders.</li>
    <li><strong>Technological (e-)crime</strong> — hacking, phishing, identity theft, online fraud. Borderless, fast-moving, and frequently never detected by the victim until long after.</li>
    <li><strong>Individual crimes</strong> — hate crime, honour crime, domestic abuse: offences against a person precisely because of who they are, typically under-reported for deeply personal reasons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The unit also requires you to analyse each type along the same four axes: what criminal
    offence (if any) is committed, who the victims are, who the offenders are, and — the
    axis that ties Unit 1 together — the <strong>level of public awareness</strong>. The
    spread is enormous. Domestic abuse, after decades of campaigns and legal reform, is now
    loudly visible even though reporting remains partial; corporate fraud may cost the
    economy billions yet barely registers as "crime" in the public mind, because its victims
    are scattered shareholders and its offender a respectable logo. Awareness is not a
    by-product of harm; it is manufactured by reporting, recording and media — which is
    exactly why the next two sections matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Why crimes go unreported — and what that does</h2>
  <p>
    The unit divides the reasons in two. <strong>Personal reasons</strong>: fear (of the
    offender, or of not being believed), shame, disinterest, or feeling not really affected.
    <strong>Social and cultural reasons</strong>: lack of knowledge that a crime occurred or
    was a crime, complexity ("who do I even tell?"), lack of media interest, lack of current
    public concern — and culture-bound crimes such as honour killing or accusations of
    witchcraft, where reporting would betray the community itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequences cut both ways. Unreported crime breeds a <em>ripple effect</em> (one
    offender's harm spreads through victims, families, neighbourhoods), lets behaviour
    <em>decriminalise</em> in practice, skews <em>police prioritisation</em>, and swells the
    pool of <em>unrecorded crime</em>. But the unit also asks for the positives: silence can
    precede <em>cultural change</em> (what was shamed becomes reportable — as with domestic
    abuse), and under-reporting can drive <em>legal and procedural change</em> once the dark
    figure is finally exposed.
  </p>

  <h2>The media's version of crime</h2>
  <p>
    Newspapers, television, film, electronic gaming, social media and music all portray
    crime — fictionally and factually — and the portrayal is systematically unrepresentative:
    violent and sexual offences are over-shown, the common crimes under-shown. Stanley
    Cohen's <em>Folk Devils and Moral Panics</em> (1972) supplies the classic mechanism:
    exaggerated reporting creates a folk devil, public concern spikes, and responses and
    punishments escalate beyond what the trend justifies. Media representation feeds
    directly into perceptions of crime trends, stereotyping of offenders, and changing
    priorities — which is why AC1.5 asks you to explain the impact with theory, not anecdote.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring the unmeasurable</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the measurement gap, step by step — why no single number can be 'the' crime rate" widget="equationStep" config={gapCfg} />
  <p>
    Because both instruments are flawed in different directions, criminologists triangulate.
    The two-hundred-year history of trying is itself a lesson in measurement: each milestone
    below created a better lens and a new kind of blind spot.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="two centuries of trying to count crime in England and Wales" widget="timeline" config={measureTimeline} />

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WJEC Level 3 Applied Diploma in Criminology, Unit 1 (AC1.1–AC1.6), 2022 specification; ONS, Crime Survey for England and Wales methodology; Home Office, National Crime Recording Standard (2002); Cohen, Folk Devils and Moral Panics (1972).</p>
</TopicPage>
